  <sharedRules>
    <rule id="65f6-1d2d-6098-ac59" name="Later Saxon Kingdoms (AoA2) - List Notes" publicationId="fcb9-17be-94c2-bcfc" hidden="false">
      <description>ARMIES OF ANTIQUITY v2 #69 (unofficial, Martin Gibbins). The later Saxon armies fought mainly on foot in a shieldwall, having relatively few missile troops and usually relegating the horse to a mode of transport to the battlefield. The kingdoms were well organised and wealthy, and could produce a levy of decently armed and competent troops, the Fyrd, which would form up with their social betters, the Thegns, to the fore. From 800 AD England was beset by both raiding and invading forces of Vikings, which were largely successful; despite a spirited fight back by Wessex under Alfred the Great, the Northern half of the country fell under Scandinavian rule. In the ninth century all this ground was retaken, only for the entire country to be conquered by Swein Forkbeard and his son Cnut between 992 and 1016. Scandinavian rule lasted until 1042 and resulted in changes to the composition of the army, with a reduction in the enthusiasm of the Fyrd and t 

ARMY COMPOSITION:
[Later Saxon Kingdoms]
  Characters: up to 33%
  Infantry: at least 50%
  Special Troops: up to 25%
  Characters: Up to 33%, reduced to 25% if the army includes huscarls. Infantry: At least 50% Special Troops: Up to 25% All spear-armed troops in the army should be armed with the same type of spear.</description>
    </rule>
  </sharedRules>
</catalogue>
